 ],
 "literal": "Fresh flowers of gold-like tanner's cassia closely packed / in a garland of many threads, on a spirited horse made of palmyra / adorned bells ringing, having mounted, modesty having destroyed / ruining affliction and inner pain continuously to increase / 'This woman did this' saying, standing in front / she, blame speaks, this village / there because of realizing, here to go a way I have indeed.",
 "translation": "I will mount the spirited horse made of palmyra stems, woven with many-threaded garlands of fresh, gold-like tanner's cassia flowers, while its adorned bells ring. Casting aside my modesty, as my ruining affliction and inner pain grow more and more, I will go. Let this village stand before me and slander her, saying, 'She is the one who caused this!' Since they will realize my love there, I have a way to go from here.",
 "modern": "பொன்னைப் போன்ற ஆவிரையின் புதிய மலர்கள் நெருங்கிய பல நூல்களால் தொடுக்கப்பட்ட மாலையை அணிந்த, பனை மடலால் செய்யப்பட்ட குதிரையின் மேல், கட்டப்பட்ட மணிகள் ஒலிக்க ஏறி, எனது நாணத்தை ஒழித்து, நெஞ்சை அழிக்கும் துன்பமும் உள்நோயும் மேலும் மேலும் வளர, 'இவள்தான் எனக்கு இதனைச் செய்தாள்' என்று இவ்வூர் மக்கள் என் முன்னின்று அவளைப் பழித்துப் பேசட்டும். அவ்வாறு அவர்கள் என் காதலை உணர்ந்து கொள்வார்கள் என்பதால், நான் இங்கிருந்து செல்வதற்கான வழி எனக்கு உண்டாயிற்று.",
 "explanation": "This poem belongs to the Kuriñci tiṇai (mountainous landscape) and is spoken by the hero. The hero is deeply in love with the heroine, but her family or the village has not yet consented to their union. In classical Tamil love poetry, when a lover's grief becomes unbearable, he threatens to perform maṭalēṟutal—mounting a mock horse made of rough palmyra stems decorated with tanner's cassia flowers, and riding it through the village to publicize his love. By doing this, he casts away his modesty to let his inner pain be known. He welcomes the village's gossip and blame directed at the heroine, because once the village realizes the depth of his love, it will pave the way for their union.",
